{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's defense fails because it conflates 'maximizing potential for learning' with a rational basis for 'preference to act.' Popper explicitly rejects the idea that surviving tests makes a theory more likely to be true or successful in the future. If the preference is merely a methodological gamble with no predictive warrant, as the target admits, then preferring Theory A over Theory B for practical action (e.g., building a bridge) is irrational unless one assumes the future will resemble the past—the very principle of induction Popper rejects. The target claims the preference is 'defensible,' but provides no non-inductive reason why an agent should bet on the better-corroborated theory rather than flipping a coin, since both have zero logical probability of succeeding next time.","premise":"Popper holds that corroboration is not probability and offers no logical guarantee that a well-tested theory will succeed in the future.","premises_essential":["2fdefdd0150ceb952efeb6d90b240d649a2c0412f664c77abc2615a5364751cb"],"successor_question":"If there is no rational link between past corroboration and future success in Popper's framework, what specific non-inductive criterion distinguishes a 'rational preference' from an arbitrary guess when lives are at stake?","target":"2fdefdd0150ceb952efeb6d90b240d649a2c0412f664c77abc2615a5364751cb"}]}